data.content:
Here's the working program (`PersonJson/Program.cs`):

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;

var person = new Person("Alice", 30);

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase,
    WriteIndented = true
};

string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
Console.WriteLine(json);

public record Person(string name, int age);
```

**Output:**
```json
{
  "Name": "Alice",
  "Age": 30
}
```

`JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` is a built-in policy that converts the record's generated property names to PascalCase. Since C# positional records already synthesize PascalCase properties (`Name`, `Age`) from the constructor parameters, the default (no policy) would also produce the same result here — but setting `PascalCase` explicitly makes the intent clear and protects you if you ever mix in camelCase-named properties.
